{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And it shall come to pass, when he hears, etc.</b> That is, afterward, when he hears the words of the curse that Israel accepted — <b>and he blesses himself</b> — he will think in his heart — <b>peace shall be to me</b> — that is, what do I care about this affliction? I did not accept the curse, and if so — <b>I will walk in the stubbornness of my heart, to add the watered</b> — that is, he will wish to add the sins that are 'watered' and for which one does not swear that there is in them a prohibition, such as wearing kilayim — <b>with the thirsty</b> — with the sins that he craves and thirsts for, such as theft and forbidden relations. But now that you all enter the covenant and accept the curses and the oaths, you will know that one who violates the covenant — <b>HaShem will not be willing to forgive him, for then HaShem's anger and His jealousy shall smoke</b> — that you have stipulated to Him to stand, and you do not stand. And from this you will not come to these evil thoughts, and you will stand in His covenant. For if you do not stand in His covenant — <b>all this curse shall lie upon him, and HaShem shall blot out his name</b> — the name of the transgressor — from under the heaven; he has no descendant and no grandchild among his people. <b>And HaShem shall separate him for evil</b> — he thinks to separate himself from Israel to walk in the stubbornness of his heart, and he will be separated for evil to himself."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:32.647124278Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}